# cpp-asset colour lookup table, format v1.
#
# 256 entries, one per line, in input order: entry i is the output for an
# input channel value of i. Each entry is three decimal integers 0..=255,
# separated by whitespace, in red green blue order. Blank lines and lines
# whose first non-space character is '#' are ignored; everything else must
# parse, and there must be exactly 256 entries.
#
# The table is applied per channel and never across channels:
#   out.r = table[in.r].r   out.g = table[in.g].g   out.b = table[in.b].b
#
# This one is sepia: the usual sepia matrix, pre-applied to a neutral grey,
# which is why it is meant to follow a `grayscale` step. Generated as
#   r = min(255, floor(i * 1.351 + 0.5))
#   g = min(255, floor(i * 1.203 + 0.5))
#   b = min(255, floor(i * 0.937 + 0.5))
#
# The 256 entries below are byte-identical to
# examples/plugins/rust-asset/luts/sepia.lut, deliberately: a grant is a
# directory and each guest can only read its own, so the only way a `lut`
# step can be compared across guests is for both tables to hold the same
# numbers. crates/host/tests/asset_e2e.rs asserts that they do.
